>x all
stool: It’s a portable folding stool, or diphros okladias.
chisel: This is a sharp tool used for carving.

iron key: It is an iron key.

ball-peen hammer: It’s a hammer used for shaping and hardening metal.

mallet: It’s a wooden hammer with a large head, used to strike a chisel.

net: It is a long wooden pole with a bag attached to one end.  This versatile
tool can be used as a quarterstaff, butterfly net, pool net, or walking stick.

wicker cage: It’s a knee-high wicker cage with an iron padlock.  It’s open.

bronze key: It is a bronze key.  On it is etched the word “rhimon”.

calculator: The calculator is [[built in]]to the wall beside the sink.  It has
buttons for all the standard unary and binary operations.  The screen reads
+20000 = 20000.

side table: Upon closer inspection, you see that the table is bracketed to the
wall.  On the side table are a manual and a comb.

manual: [[Operator]]’s Manual

To control the auto-sink, use the calculator add-on to enter the desired volume
of water.  It supports the standard Thalassa++ unary and binary operations:

   + – addition
   - – subtraction
   * – multiplication
   / – division
   % – modulo
   ~ – bitwise NOT
   ! – logical NOT
   & – bitwise AND
   | – bitwise OR
   ^ – bitwise XOR
   << – shift left
   >> – arithmetic shift right
   >>> – logical shift right

For example,
       CALCULATE 69 TIMES 105
to fill the sink with 7,245 kochliaria.

Warning: Do not use big [[number]]s or divide by zero!

comb: It’s a tool to keep your hair tidy.

sink: This is a state-of-the-art mop sink with anti-miasmic coating and bronze
backsplash.  It is so modern, there are no handles or other obvious ways to
turn it on.

It is full to the brim with water.

A needle and an idol are [[float]]ing on the surface of the water.

needle: This is a sharp tool used for sewing.  It is made of silver.

idol: It’s a small statuette of Aphrodite carved from meerschaum.

